From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mathieu GRENET <mathieu@mgth.fr>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 09:33:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 34/42] ad_orender: let the engine decode on a thread of its
 own when the user asks

The engine (Omniphony, ABI 0.11) can decode on a thread of its own,
following the user's `decode_thread` option from Studio or config.yaml.
With it on, a packet's audio comes back a few calls after the packet, so a
host has to cope with that before the engine may do it. This decoder now
does, and hands the choice to the user:

- On an engine that reports each packet's timestamp with its audio
  (`orender_output_packet_pts`, ABI >= 11), it sets `decode_thread=live`.
  Older engines are left alone.
- Output is stamped with the timestamp of the packet the audio came from,
  instead of the one just passed in. Inline that is the same packet, so
  nothing changes when the option is off. Input timestamps travel as
  microseconds, rounded, with INT64_MIN standing for "none".
- At EOF, the EOF is held back while the engine is drained, a packet's
  audio per pass, and passed on once the engine has nothing left. A seek
  (reset) during the drain discards what was left.
- The loader resolves `orender_drain` and `orender_output_packet_pts` as
  optional symbols with stubs.
- The vendored header moves to ABI 0.11 (mgth/Omniphony@fbe5b0d1).

Checked on a 30 s TrueHD and a 30 s E-AC-3 sample:
- PCM output with the option off and on is byte-identical to the
  previous mpv, full length.
- Output timestamps match the previous mpv's to within the run-to-run
  jitter the FIR crossover's latency update already has (2.6 ms, once).
- Toggling the option over OSC mid-playback leaves no timestamp gap.
- Without the per-packet timestamp, output lands 32 ms late on E-AC-3.
  Without the drain, the last 18 ms (TrueHD) and 32 ms (E-AC-3) are lost.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit aebdcc6197996364113f27dd17d0f7c0730cc653)
